WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.980
Speaker 1: The January 6th committee doesn't have&nbsp;
much time left because Republicans are taking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:11.940
over the House in January. And Republican Adam&nbsp;
Kinzinger now says that Donald Trump absolutely is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:19.800
guilty of crimes committed in advance of January&nbsp;
6th. There's a report from the Hill. Congressman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:24.000
Adam Kinzinger, Republican from Illinois, said&nbsp;
Wednesday that former President Trump is, quote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:30.600
absolutely guilty of a crime surrounding the&nbsp;
January six riot at the Capitol. I think he's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:36.060
guilty of a crime. This is a quote. I mean, look,&nbsp;
he knew what he did. We've made that clear. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:41.520
knew what was happening prior to January 6th. He&nbsp;
pressured the Justice Department officials to say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:46.740
hey, just say the election was stolen and&nbsp;
leave the rest to me. And then the Republicans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:51.840
all need to put the stamp of approval on it.&nbsp;
Kinzinger told CNN's Jake TAPPER on the lead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:59.040
quote, I think he is absolutely guilty if he is&nbsp;
not guilty of some crime, of some kind of a crime.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:03.660
I mean, what we've basically said is presidents&nbsp;
are above the law and they can do everything short&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:10.800
of a coup as long as it doesn't succeed. Kinzinger&nbsp;
said, though he gave the caveat that even as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:16.680
member of the January six panel, he doesn't&nbsp;
have the DOJ's power to press charges. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:25.020
that we know you could make the case that&nbsp;
over the four years ending on January six,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:31.680
2021 or January 10th, 2021, Donald Trump&nbsp;
committed dozens of counts of obstruction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:40.440
Put that aside from possible sedition based&nbsp;
on his actions. Myriad conspiracy charges of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:46.260
all kinds associated with the Republican coup&nbsp;
attempt, mishandling of classified documents,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:51.720
taking classified documents. And then in addition&nbsp;
to that minimum on the criminal side, think about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:58.800
all of the potential civil claims that could be&nbsp;
made. Reminder that is important, which is if and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:06.360
until we have reason to revise our belief. It is&nbsp;
pretty widely known that the Department of Justice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:13.260
to some degree accepts that the president is&nbsp;
above the law, not in a literal sense. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:19.740
there is this convention that has taken place. And&nbsp;
until we actually see the DOJ behave differently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:25.980
we don't really have a reason to revise that&nbsp;
perspective. So the January six committee will be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:31.020
meeting next week. They announced that yesterday.&nbsp;
It's a it's described as a business meeting.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:35.760
Business for them is a little bit different than&nbsp;
what we consider to be business. But presumably,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:40.860
they will be meeting to make a decision as&nbsp;
to what, if any, criminal referrals they are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:46.320
going to make. The news from last week, the&nbsp;
January 6th committee will be making criminal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:52.260
referrals. Will they in there, in fact, do&nbsp;
that and against whom? Remains to be seen.

